            <WaterConservation LowFlushToilets="2" LowFlushToiletsUpgrade="2" />
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

Overall, your house is quite air tight, however we did still identify a few locations that can be addressed:



** The trim around the new front entry door system as well as the window trim around some of the newer windows (primarily the ones in the main floor front bedroom &amp; rear clothes room, and the 3 basement windows behind the couch)



** There is a 3" chimney for the current hot water tank that will be eliminated if you decide to install an on-demand hot water heater.



Savings: The software models a modest 10% reduction in overall air leakage, that could potentially save $ 56 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 2.0 GJ.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls />
                <ceilings />
                <ceilingsCathedral />
                <exposedFloors />
                <foundation />
                <windows />
                <doors />
                <VentilationSystem>

As outlined in the Air Sealing section of this report, your house is already quite air tight &amp; will become a little tighter as you air seal some of those pesky air leaks around the new windows/doors and perhaps install a new on-demand hot water heater.



If your house was being built today (including the renovation upgrades you have completed), it would have a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) installed.



Another way for you to increase ventilation whenever you feel it is necessary (when indoor air feels stale/stuffy, or when condensation may by gathering on the bottom edge of the new windows), you can easily do this by:



1. Having your heating contractor install an outside fresh air supply pipe connected to the return plenum of the furnace, with an integrated inline damper that you can manually adjust



2. Install a timer switch on the main floor bathroom exhaust fan that you can program.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

You currently have a 151 litre propane hot water tank that is vented up a 3" chimney and only operates at an Energy Factor of about 0.59 (this means about 59% efficiency). I have modeled an option for you to consider replacing this hot water heater with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an Energy Factor of 0.92 or higher.



Savings: By replacing your current hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR?????? qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of a minimum 0.92 EF, could potentially save $ 184 per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by 6.9 GJ, and qualify for up to $750 in rebates from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program (EEIP).



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



NOTE: The GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program is regularly reviewed and the rebate amount and/or eligibility criteria can change. In addition, rebate funding is provided on a first-come, first-serve basis contingent upon available funding. Please check our website before purchasing to determine current rebate amounts, eligibility criteria and if rebate funding is still available.

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem />
                <Renewables />
                <WaterConservation />
                <AdditionalComments>

LED Lights



Savings: Light Emitting Diode (LED) lights save about 80% compared to regular incandescent lighting, and about 20% compared to flourescent lighting. Floursecent lighting also contains mercury which is a hazardous waste material that requires special disposal. And ..... LED lights work well in the cold and can last a long time.



Rebates: In Hay River, LED light bulbs, tubes and integrated fixtures are eligible for a rebate of 50% of the purchase price up to $ 200 (per fiscal year)  from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within three months of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Depressurization Test



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment that vents to the outside (for your house, only the 2 bathroom fans and the clothes dryer as the kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside ), indicated a pressure drop of 2.0 Pascals. As this number is less than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment is not currently expected to draw combustion products into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is direct vent and not susceptible to back-drafting. The hot water tank and the wood stove are both chimney vented and can be susceptible to back-drafting. If you upgrade to an on-demand hot water heater &amp; install a dedicated outside combustion air pipe (directly connected to the back of the wood stove). the potential for back-drafting will be significantly reduced.



During my assessment of your home, I noticed both smoke and carbon monoxide (CO) detectors. Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Ventilation



Your home currently has no exhaust ventilation:

        - The kitchen range hood does not vent to the outside

        - 2 bathroom fans

        - A clothes dryer



You may want to upgrade the bathroom exhaust fans to new ENERGY STAR rated bathroom exhaust fans that use little electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels - and they have good internal dampers to reduce heat loss when they are not in use. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Fuel Costs - Hay River



The following Nov. 1, 2020 values, including GST, have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Hay River:

Electricity - $19.66 min, $0.33 per kWh up to monthly subsidy threshold, $0.37 per kWh thereafter

Oil  - $0.80 per litre 

Propane - $0.66 per litre delivered, $2.46 per m3 piped to the house

Wood - $315.00 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.20 per bag, $268.12 per tonne bulk delivered



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Disclaimer



The foregoing report is provided as an assessment of the visible components only and shall not be taken as a guarantee or an acceptance of liability for future performance of any part of the building.  This report is prepared with reasonable skill and care within the limitations of a visual inspection on the inspection date. This evaluation and report do not cover code compliance issues set by the authority having jurisdiction.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



Your Energy Advisor



Please do not hesitate to contact me if you have any questions.



Sincerely,



Ken Baigent, Senior Energy Management Specialist

Arctic Energy Alliance

Phone: (867) 920-3333

Fax: (867) 873-0303

E-mail: Ken.Baigent@aea.nt.ca



Arctic Energy Alliance: Taking action on high heating and power bills, high trans</AdditionalComments>
